Admin Console - Bug Fixes and UI Enhancements
	1.	Add a new column to the User Accounts table showing a quick-glance summary of what each user has access to (which applications and which jobs). This should be visible directly in the list, in addition to or in place of the current per-row Access link. If the full access list is too long to show inline, use a condensed summary (such as a count) with a hover or click-to-expand detail.
	2.	There is an existing shared, reusable scrollbar component: a thin, pill-shaped scrollbar with no visible background, that only appears on mouse movement and supports click-and-drag scrolling. The Admin Console is not using this shared component. When the panel is resized to a narrower width and content overflows vertically, a default browser scrollbar appears instead. This keeps happening repeatedly across new screens. Audit every scrollable container in the Admin Console, replace any default scrollbar with the shared component, and investigate why new screens keep failing to reuse this logic so it stops recurring across the app, not just here.
	3.	Fix user creation validation based on role. Admin role requires no application or job selection, since admin has full access by default; keep this as is. DevOps role requires only application selection with no job-level selection; keep this as is. For developer and tester roles: require at least one application to be selected before the user can be created (these roles can still be granted access to multiple applications, just not zero). If no application is selected, block creation and show a validation error, even if jobs are selected. If one or more applications are selected but no specific jobs are selected, allow creation and grant the user access to all jobs under the selected application or applications by default. If a job is selected without selecting an application, block creation and show a validation error asking the user to select an application first.
	4.	Change the job selection in the user creation flow so jobs are scoped to the selected application. Right now every job across every application is shown in one flat list, which is confusing for anyone unfamiliar with which job belongs to which application. After username, password, and role are entered, show the application selector first. Once an application (or applications) is selected, populate the jobs list with only the jobs that actually belong to those selected applications, and let the user pick which of those jobs to grant.
	5.	Modernize the Admin Console UI while keeping the current two-group structure of Job Access and User Accounts. Instead of expanding each group inline as an accordion, make the initial view show the two groups as selectable entries; selecting one should navigate to a dedicated full view for that group with a smooth transition animation, plus a back button to return to the group selection view. Keep the existing close button to exit the Admin Console entirely. Refresh the overall visual style (borders, spacing, typography) to be modern and minimalistic, consistent with the rest of the app.
	6.	Replace the enable and disable toggle switches on the Job Access screen with the existing shared, reusable toggle component already used elsewhere in the app (styled similar to GitHub Actions toggles), instead of the current inconsistent implementation. Add clear status icons to indicate the enabled and disabled states.
